# lccc-only: __builtin_cpu_supports folds at COMPILE TIME against the
# invocation's code-generation permission (AUD-CPU-1; the old hard-coded
# Raptor Lake allowlist is gone) because the fold must never promise an
# instruction the backend was not allowed to emit.  GCC implements runtime
# CPUID instead, so on any host that is not the assumed CPU — this regression
# VM is an AVX-512-capable Xeon — GCC correctly returns true for "avx512f" and
# the oracle binary exits 7, failing the test's compile-time assumption.
# Host-independent lccc vs host-dependent GCC is not a valid differential pair
# for this test; the lccc self-check is the gate, and
# tests/regression/check_cpu_supports_fold.sh covers the -maes /
# -march=raptorlake half that grants AES and PCLMUL.
LCCC_NO_COMPARE=1
